  "ts-drawing-009": {
   "d": "ts-drawing",
   "tag": "ts-drawing",
   "q": "Triethylamine reacts with iodoethane by {{SN2}}. Which partial charges belong on the transition state?\n[[rxn:CCN(CC)CC + CCI>>CC[N+](CC)(CC)CC.[I-]]]",
   "ok": "δ+ on N and δ- on I",
   "wrong": [
    "δ- on N and δ- on I",
    "δ- on N and δ+ on I",
    "No charges, because both starting materials are neutral"
   ],
   "ex": "The lecture's transition state for this reaction. Nitrogen is losing a share of its lone pair and iodine is gaining the bonding pair. <b>Answer:</b> δ+ on N and δ- on I.",
   "why": {
    "δ- on N and δ- on I": "This is the pattern for an anionic nucleophile. Triethylamine is neutral, and two δ- would not add up to zero.",
    "δ- on N and δ+ on I": "Reversed. The nucleophile gives electrons away and the leaving group takes them.",
    "No charges, because both starting materials are neutral": "The products are ions, so charge must be developing in the transition state."
   }
  },
  "solvent-effects-010": {
   "d": "solvent-effects",
   "tag": "solvent-effects",
   "q": "In a less polar solvent, the reaction of triethylamine with iodoethane has this energy curve:\n[[energy:R=0,TS=24,P=-4|R=reactants 0,TS=24,P=products −4]]\nWhich curve fits the same reaction in a <b>more polar</b> solvent, on the same energy scale?",
   "ok": "[[energy:R=-1,TS=18,P=-12|R=reactants −1,TS=18,P=products −12]]",
   "wrong": [
    "[[energy:R=-8,TS=22,P=-6|R=reactants −8,TS=22,P=products −6]]",
    "[[energy:R=0,TS=24,P=-12|R=reactants 0,TS=24,P=products −12]]",
    "[[energy:R=0,TS=30,P=-4|R=reactants 0,TS=30,P=products −4]]"
   ],
   "ex": "The neutral start barely moves, the partly charged transition state drops, and the ionic product drops most. The barrier falls from 24 to 19. <b>Answer:</b> The curve that starts at about the same level, with a lower top and a lower product.",
   "why": {
    "[[energy:R=-8,TS=22,P=-6|R=reactants −8,TS=22,P=products −6]]": "Here the starting materials drop the most. That is the pattern for charged starting materials, not neutral ones.",
    "[[energy:R=0,TS=24,P=-12|R=reactants 0,TS=24,P=products −12]]": "Only the product is lowered. The transition state already carries partial charges, so it must drop as well.",
    "[[energy:R=0,TS=30,P=-4|R=reactants 0,TS=30,P=products −4]]": "A polar solvent does not raise the energy of a transition state that has charge on it."
   }
  },
  "solvent-effects-012": {
   "d": "solvent-effects",
   "tag": "solvent-effects",
   "q": "Each reaction is moved to a more polar solvent. Which one becomes <b>slower</b>?",
   "ok": "[[rxn:[OH-] + C[S+](C)C>>CO + CSC]]",
   "wrong": [
    "[[rxn:CCN(CC)CC + CCI>>CC[N+](CC)(CC)CC.[I-]]]",
    "[[rxn:CC(C)(C)Br>H2O;>CC(C)(C)O]]",
    "[[rxn:c1ccncc1 + CI>>C[n+]1ccccc1.[I-]]]"
   ],
   "ex": "Both reactants are ions. Their charge is disappearing in the transition state, so a more polar solvent favours the starting materials and raises the barrier. <b>Answer:</b> Hydroxide with the trimethylsulfonium ion.",
   "why": {
    "[[rxn:CCN(CC)CC + CCI>>CC[N+](CC)(CC)CC.[I-]]]": "Neutral reactants and a transition state with δ+ and δ-: faster in the more polar solvent.",
    "[[rxn:CC(C)(C)Br>H2O;>CC(C)(C)O]]": "{{SN1}}: the neutral substrate ionizes, so more polarity means a faster reaction.",
    "[[rxn:c1ccncc1 + CI>>C[n+]1ccccc1.[I-]]]": "The same type as the triethylamine reaction: two neutral molecules making charge."
   }
  },
  "solvent-effects-008": {
   "d": "solvent-effects",
   "tag": "solvent-effects",
   "q": "Sodium azide reacts with bromoethane much more slowly in ethanol than in DMF. What is the main reason?\n[[rxn:CCBr + [N-]=[N+]=[N-].[Na+]>>CCN=[N+]=[N-] + [Na+].[Br-]]]",
   "ok": "Ethanol hydrogen-bonds to the azide ion, which stabilizes the nucleophile and raises the barrier",
   "wrong": [
    "Ethanol is too nonpolar to dissolve sodium azide",
    "DMF hydrogen-bonds to the bromide and pulls it off",
    "In DMF the reaction changes to {{SN1}}"
   ],
   "ex": "A solvated anion is a stabilized starting material. The transition state, with its charge spread out, is stabilized less. <b>Answer:</b> Ethanol hydrogen-bonds to the azide ion and stabilizes it.",
   "why": {
    "Ethanol is too nonpolar to dissolve sodium azide": "Ethanol is a polar solvent. The problem is the hydrogen bonds it makes to the anion.",
    "DMF hydrogen-bonds to the bromide and pulls it off": "DMF has no O-H or N-H and cannot donate a hydrogen bond.",
    "In DMF the reaction changes to {{SN1}}": "A primary substrate does not ionize. It is {{SN2}} in both solvents, only faster in DMF."
   }
  },
