  "zaitsev-hofmann-015": {
   "d": "zaitsev-hofmann",
   "tag": "zaitsev-hofmann",
   "q": "On the exam, trans-1-chloro-2-methylcyclohexane with {{NaOCH3}} in {{CH3OH}} gave 3-methylcyclohexene, the less substituted alkene, as the major product. What is the reason?\n[[rxn:C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Cl>NaOCH3;CH3OH>C[C@@H]1C=CCCC1]]",
   "ok": "The hydrogen needed for the more substituted alkene cannot be anti-periplanar to Cl",
   "wrong": [
    "Chloride is a Hofmann leaving group",
    "Methoxide is a bulky base",
    "The reaction is E1, and the carbocation loses the most accessible hydrogen"
   ],
   "ex": "In the chair with Cl axial, the trans methyl group is axial, so the hydrogen on its carbon is equatorial. Only the other neighbour has an axial hydrogen. Geometry rules the Saytzeff alkene out. <b>Answer:</b> Geometry: the hydrogen that would give the Saytzeff alkene cannot be anti-periplanar to Cl.",
   "why": {
    "Chloride is a Hofmann leaving group": "In the lecture's table chloride gives 67% Saytzeff product. Only fluoride is a Hofmann leaving group.",
    "Methoxide is a bulky base": "Methoxide is the smallest alkoxide. With open-chain bromides and chlorides it gives the Saytzeff alkene.",
    "The reaction is E1, and the carbocation loses the most accessible hydrogen": "Methoxide is a strong base, so the reaction is E2. An E1 reaction would in any case favour the more substituted alkene."
   },
   "keep": 2
  },
  "williamson-why-005": {
   "d": "williamson-why",
   "tag": "williamson-why",
   "q": "A student tries to make tert-butyl methyl ether this way. What does the flask contain at the end?\n[[rxn:CC(C)(C)Br + C[O-].[Na+]>CH3OH>\"?\"]]",
   "ok": "[[mol:CC(C)=C]], [[mol:CO]] and [[mol:[Br-]]]",
   "wrong": [
    "[[mol:CC(C)(C)OC]] and [[mol:[Br-]]]",
    "[[mol:CC(C)(C)O]] and [[mol:[Br-]]]",
    "Unchanged starting materials"
   ],
   "ex": "{{E2}}: methoxide takes a beta hydrogen and becomes methanol, the C-H electrons make the C=C, bromide leaves. <b>Answer:</b> 2-Methylpropene, methanol and bromide ion.",
   "why": {
    "[[mol:CC(C)(C)OC]] and [[mol:[Br-]]]": "This would need {{SN2}} at a tertiary carbon, which does not happen: three methyl groups block the back of the carbon.",
    "[[mol:CC(C)(C)O]] and [[mol:[Br-]]]": "There is no water or hydroxide in the flask to supply an OH, and a strong base is present, so the halide does not wait to ionize.",
    "Unchanged starting materials": "Blocking {{SN2}} does not stop the reaction. The nine beta hydrogens are on the outside of the molecule, easy for a strong base to reach."
   }
  },
  "route-choice-011": {
   "d": "route-choice",
   "tag": "route-choice",
   "q": "The target is the single enantiomer shown, (R)-2-methoxybutane. Circle the route that gives it cleanly.\n[[mol:CC[C@@H](C)OC]]",
   "ok": "[[rxn:CC[C@@H](C)[O-].[Na+] + CI>DMSO>CC[C@@H](C)OC]]",
   "wrong": [
    "[[rxn:CC[C@H](C)Br + C[O-].[Na+]>CH3OH>CC[C@@H](C)OC]]"
   ],
   "ex": "{{SN2}} at the methyl carbon. No bond to the stereocentre is made or broken, so the (R) alkoxide gives the (R) ether, and a methyl halide cannot eliminate. <b>Answer:</b> The (R) alkoxide with iodomethane in DMSO.",
   "why": {
    "[[rxn:CC[C@H](C)Br + C[O-].[Na+]>CH3OH>CC[C@@H](C)OC]]": "On paper, {{SN2}} on the (S) bromide would invert to the (R) ether. But methoxide is a strong base and the halide is secondary: {{E2}} to butenes is the main reaction."
   }
  },
